@jbrockmendel the NEP is really well laid out and goes over the memory footprint as well as comparison to PyArrow:

https://numpy.org/neps/nep-0055-string_dtype.html#memory-layout-examples

Unfortunately looks to be a different layout so I think there would have to be a copy. @ngoldbaum may have more insights though

That section is current specifically about `pd.StringDtype()` and `pd.ArrowDtype(pa.string())` is not one of the StringDtype instantiations, but it would probably be good to also mention ArrowDtype as yet another way that you can have a string column (although note that it is not just `pd.ArrowDtype(pa.string())`, but also `pd.ArrowDtype(pa.large_string())`, and string_view ..)

> I was under the intention that the migration guide is aimed at users not doing so.

@rhshadrach yes, but that is not necessarily clear to such users? So that was my idea for a brief section targeted at such users, to essentially say that if you are already opt-ing to one of the nullable ones, you can keep doing that and nothing should change.

@michiel-de-muynck 
Could you confirm what would be the expected behavior when 
```
import pandas as pd
from pandas.testing import assert_frame_equal

df1 = pd.DataFrame(
    {
        "x": pd.Series([pd.NA,1], dtype="Int32"),
    }
)
df2 = pd.DataFrame(
    {
        "x": pd.Series([pd.NA,1], dtype="object"),
    }
)

assert_frame_equal(df1, df2, check_dtype=False) # fails, but should succeed
```


Thanks.

We're going to need to add to `ArrowEA.__array_ufunc__` something like:

```
if not nan_is_na() and ufunc is np.isnan:
    if self.dtype.kind == "f":
        return pc.is_nan(self._pa_array).to_numpy()
    return np.zeros(self.shape, dtype=bool)
```

@jorisvandenbossche 

> Quite likely we might end up using pyarrow for almost all dtypes, I just don't think being "strict" about this should be our goal.

yes, we have funding that can be used for a strict outcome, but that doesn't mean we have to go that route if the team in not in agreement. However, taking the scenic route is currently not funded. If the pyarrow world is not approved by the team, IIUC the sponsors are happy with a global flag that allows existing pandas code to just work with the "pyarrow dtypes". This is currently a separate issue #61620 to discuss that and we would probably end up with some dtype mapping (like PDEP-13, but not blocked by it) and some other breaking changes that apply to that dtype system only, like ignoring dtype_backend and always using "pyarrow". Comparing performance etc is not relevant on this more direct journey since the jit compilers have already been optimized for Arrow types and the 1d nature of the arrays is not relevant.

> For the workgroup, @Dr-Irv is the expert for what I know, maybe it can advice if this group should simply be removed or what's the plan with the new governance. Or if the CI and web rendering doesn't break, maybe just leave it empty. Also ok to put me there to get this merged, and we see later if needed.
> 

It's on my list to update the various teams in a few places.

For that workgroup (communications), include the following people:

@WillAyd Will Ayd
@rhshadrach Richard Shadrach
@jorisvandenbossche Joris Van den Bossche

1) If a different group asked for the same thing I'd _also_ tell them to do `whatever.apply(df, func)`

2) If there were to be a "standardized entry point" I would advocate for it to be

```
def apply(self, func, engine=None):
    if engine is not None and engine != "numba":
        importlib.import(engine)
        return engine.apply(func, self)
    [...]  
```

3) My main objection is that adding this makes us responsible for maintaining it, which we are not in a position to do.  If we want to change something non-public and it breaks `df.apply(func, engine="bodo")` we are up a creek.

Sorry, you are correct. I didn't realize the problem with the floating point precision.

I wonder what's the impact on performance of this change, did you execute a benchmark to compare the execution times of both implementations?

@MarcoGorelli @jbrockmendel thoughts on this change?

@mroeschke could it not return [`boolean`](https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/user_guide/boolean.html)?

The page you linked to does call it out a little later on:
https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/blob/56b5979f136e72ce78e5221392739481c025d5e7/doc/source/user_guide/missing_data.rst?plain=1#L32-L33

@mohsinm-dev - it does seem like the right direction. But I think we can avoid factorize by calling `dropna` on the unique values. Then `get_indexer` will naturally return `-1` for any missing NA values.

@zachyattack23 

> Would this be a doable task I could do in max 7 hours worth of time?

I do not think this is answerable. Partially because of the large variability in how long an issue takes different people, and partly because one can start working on a issue thinking it will be easy only for it to take a very long time.

@rhshadrach I appreciate the feedback of Spikhalskiy that in production pipelines dtypes often default to object when the result is empty. This causes round function to fail unexpectedly while other operations in the chain pass.

Since you're concerned about consistency between empty and non-empty object Series, would you be open to keeping the TypeError but making the message more actionable?

Proposed message: "TypeError: .round() is only supported for numeric dtypes. For empty Series (e.g. from SQL), ensure a numeric dtype is set before rounding.
